<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kotte</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte is the legislative capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, a city on the south-eastern edge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a>. As Kotte it was the capital of a Sinhalese kingdom from the fifteenth century until the late sixteenth century, when it was abandoned under Portuguese pressure. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-r-jayewardene">J. R. Jayewardene</a> revived it as the seat of government, and the new parliament building opened there in 1982.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sri-jayawardenepura-kotte.jpg" alt="Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rehman Abubakr · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diyawanna_Lake_-_October_2017_(1_of_3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Admiral Zheng He returned to Nanjing from the third treasure voyage and presented his Sinhalese captives, including their king, taken in the Ming–<b>Kotte</b> War, to the Yongle Emperor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-6">6 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Soon afterwards Parakramabahu VI took power and moved the capital to Kotte, founding the Kingdom of <b>Kotte</b> in 1412.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gampola">Kingdom of Gampola</a></li><li><blockquote>He moved the capital to the fortified town of <b>Kotte</b> near Colombo, which the Alakeshvaras had built as a defence against the Jaffna Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kotte">Kingdom of Kotte</a></li><li><blockquote>Pararajasinghe, also called Raigam Bandara, received the Raigama district south of <b>Kotte</b>, while his brothers took Kotte itself and the Kingdom of Sitawaka.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-raigama">Kingdom of Raigama</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1978 J. R. Jayewardene introduced an executive presidency, and the administrative capital moved to <b>Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte</b>, near Colombo.</blockquote><a href="/state/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a></li></ul></section>
